	<!--
		Submenu seeds, one per *.DATA.xml. standalone="false" keeps the includes
		builder from also emitting a bare skinshortcuts-<name> per template
		(builders/includes.py:106); the only submenu include this skin asks for
		is the flat skinshortcuts-submenu, and templates.xml builds that one.
	-->
	<submenu name="movies" standalone="false">
		<!-- These three come first, in this order: Library, Lists, Files. Order
		     here IS render order: builders/includes.py walks the defaults in file
		     order and the override layer keys its own entries by `name`, so
		     moving an <item> reorders the strip without disturbing a box's saved
		     customisation. Never renumber a `name` for tidiness.

		     EVERYTHING AFTER THE THIRD SHIPS DISABLED. Owner decision
		     2026-08-01: a fresh install draws exactly these three tiles here,
		     not 15. Before that the whole strip shipped on and only movies-1
		     was off, which is what the previous version of this comment
		     described and it is no longer true.

		     The 15 switched-off items stay PRESENT on purpose. <disabled> is
		     not deletion: builders/includes.py:163 skips a disabled item when
		     it emits the include, and dialog/items.py toggles the same flag, so
		     every one of them is still listed in the menu editor and can be
		     switched back on there. Delete an <item> instead and it leaves the
		     editor entirely and a box that had it on is left holding an
		     override for a name that no longer exists.

		     A box that has already customised this strip is unaffected. The
		     override layer keys by `name` and userdata.py:378 prefers the
		     stored value whenever there is one, so a saved on/off decision
		     still wins over the default underneath it. No `name` changed here,
		     so nothing is orphaned. What does change for an existing box is
		     "Reset Item", which restores the default, and the default is now
		     off for everything below the third.

		     Do not read a bench render as evidence of what ships, in either
		     direction. The include builder applies the override layer before it
		     emits anything, so a bench strip is that box's saved state, not
		     this file. Read this file, or read the emitted include. -->
		<!-- movies-14 and movies-15 additionally carry a <visible> gate on
		     script.embuary.info. That gate is evaluated by Kodi at render time
		     and <disabled> is evaluated by the include builder, so the two are
		     independent: both items are omitted from the include while they are
		     disabled, and if a user switches either back on, the add-on gate
		     still decides whether it draws. It is kept for that case. -->
